#~
Lantern -- find four relics in the dark, and get out before the lamp does.

  compile: obc -src gl_lantern.obs -lib sdl2,sdl_gl
  run:     obr gl_lantern

  mouse        look around
  W A S D      walk
  shift        run
  E            take a relic you are standing next to
  click/space  throw a flare
  escape       quit

## The game

Four relics, one exit, and a lamp that is burning down the whole time.

Shades will not enter the lamp's light. They close on you until the light gets
too strong, then hold at that edge -- so the lamp is cover, and the fuel bar is
the timer on it. Above about a third of a tank they cannot reach you at all.
Below it they can, and do.

A flare is the only thing that kills one, and it costs fuel to throw. That is
the whole decision the game is built on: every shade you clear makes the rest
of the dungeon darker, and the dark is what lets the next one reach you.

Relics give fuel back, so collecting is also survival.

## Why this program exists

Every other example here demonstrates a feature. This one tries to be a small
game, which is a different test: it uses the newest parts of the framework
together, continuously, under a camera the player controls -- rather than one at
a time in a fixture that was written to make them pass.

The lantern is the point. It is a POINT LIGHT that moves with you, and it casts
shadows in every direction through a cube map. A fixture can check a cube shadow
by putting a caster on each axis and reading six pixels; it cannot check that the
six faces stay consistent with each other while the light moves, because a still
image has no way to be wrong about that. Walking a lantern down a corridor does:
if any face is rotated, mirrored, or fed the wrong view matrix, the shadows tear
or swim as you turn, and you see it immediately.

So the parts under test are: PointShadow with a moving light, Scene collision in
corridors rather than an open room, frustum culling with enough boxes to matter,
and an Overlay HUD that has to be legible over a dark scene.

The shades and the flare add three more, and they are the parts a still frame
cannot check either. Scene->Raycast has to agree with the crosshair while both
the camera and the target are moving. Props have to be identity-comparable, so
the thing the ray reports back can be matched to the shade that owns it. And the
particle burst has to appear where the ray actually struck, along the surface
normal -- if the ray and the render disagree about where that is, the sparks go
into the wall and you see it at once.

## What it could not use, and why that is the useful part

Four things this program wants and cannot have. They are marked GAP below, and
they are the argument for the next round of work rather than complaints:

  GAP 1  CLOSED. Writing this program is what established that PollEvents
         handled four event types and discarded the rest, so no mouse button
         existed anywhere in the framework and pickup had to be bound to E.
         GLWindow now reports key and button edges, the wheel and the pointer,
         and pickup is a left click -- with E kept, because a one-button game
         that also works from the keyboard costs one line.
  GAP 2  CLOSED. Rect took no colour at all, so the fuel bar was white and
         "low fuel" had to be spelled out in words. Overlay now has a tint that
         multiplies in the shader, so the bar changes colour per frame without
         re-rasterising anything -- which SetColor could not do, because it bakes
         the colour into the glyph textures and throws the cache away.
  GAP 3  CLOSED. Game.OpenGL still has no audio of its own and does not need
         any -- Game.SDL2's mixer needs no renderer. What was missing was the
         initialisation: nothing opened the audio device, so a working mixer
         looked like an absent feature. GLWindow now takes an audio flag, and
         the pickup chime below is synthesised rather than shipped, so the
         example stays asset-free.
  GAP 4  CLOSED. Lit shaders now carry an opacity and pass the texture's own
         alpha through, Material has SetOpacity, and Scene draws solid geometry
         first and transparent boxes afterwards sorted back to front. The relics
         have the glow shell this note asked for.

## The one bit of arithmetic worth reading

  fuel_light := @fuel / @fuel_max;
  @lamp->SetIntensity(0.35 + fuel_light * 0.9);

The lantern dims as it burns down, which is the whole difficulty curve: the
shadow map does not change, the light does, and the room closes in around you.
It costs two lines because Light owns intensity separately from colour.
~#

use Game.SDL2;
use Game.OpenGL;
use System.IO.Filesystem;

#~
A relic: somewhere to stand, and whether it has been taken.

This is a class rather than three parallel arrays because the alternative was
@relics[i * 4 + 2], which is how the walkthrough's room started out and is worth
not repeating.

A relic is a different mesh plus a transform, which is what Prop is for. It had
no home in the framework when this program was written and was drawn by hand;
that absence is what argued Prop into existence, and each relic is now two of
them -- a solid body and a translucent shell -- held by the Scene.
~#
class Relic {
	@at : Vector3;
	@taken : Bool;
	@spin : Float;
	# The solid relic and the translucent shell around it. Both are Props in the
	# scene, so Scene draws them, culls them, and -- because the shell is
	# transparent -- sorts it against every other transparent thing in the room.
	@body : Prop;
	@glow : Prop;

	New(x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) {
		@at := Vector3->New(x, y, z);
		@taken := false;
		@spin := 0.0;
	}

	#~
	Put this relic in the scene as two props.
	@param scene the scene to join
	@param mesh the sphere both props share -- neither owns it
	@param solid the relic's own material
	@param shell the translucent glow material
	~#
	method : public : AddTo(scene : Scene, mesh : Mesh, solid : Material, shell : Material) ~ Nil {
		@body := scene->AddProp(Prop->New(mesh, solid));
		@body->GetTransform()->SetScale(0.28);

		@glow := scene->AddProp(Prop->New(mesh, shell));
		@glow->GetTransform()->SetScale(0.44);
	}

	#~
	Advance the bob and spin, and push them into the props.

	The transform is the prop's own, so writing to it is all that is needed --
	there is no per-frame upload to arrange and nothing to draw by hand.
	~#
	method : public : Animate(delta : Float) ~ Nil {
		@spin += delta * 1.4;

		if(@body = Nil) {
			return;
		};

		# Bobbing, so a relic reads as an object in the room rather than a decal
		# on the floor. The lamp's falloff does the rest.
		bob := 0.12 * @spin->Sin();
		@body->GetTransform()->SetPosition(@at->GetX(), @at->GetY() + bob, @at->GetZ());
		@body->GetTransform()->SetRotation(@spin, 0.0, 0.0);

		# The shell turns the other way, so the two never look welded together.
		@glow->GetTransform()->SetPosition(@at->GetX(), @at->GetY() + bob, @at->GetZ());
		@glow->GetTransform()->SetRotation(@spin * -0.5, 0.0, 0.0);
	}

	method : public : GetAt() ~ Vector3 { return @at; }
	method : public : IsTaken() ~ Bool { return @taken; }
	method : public : Take() ~ Nil {
		@taken := true;
		# One call instead of an if-not-taken test in the draw loop.
		if(@body <> Nil) {
			@body->SetVisible(false);
			@glow->SetVisible(false);
		};
	}

	#~
	Distance from a point, ignoring height -- the player and the relic are both
	somewhere on the floor, and comparing their eye heights would mean the pickup
	radius changed when you looked up.
	~#
	method : public : GroundDistance(eye : Vector3) ~ Float {
		dx := @at->GetX() - eye->GetX();
		dz := @at->GetZ() - eye->GetZ();
		return (dx * dx + dz * dz)->Sqrt();
	}
}


#~
A shade: something in the dark that wants to be where you are.

The rule that makes this a game rather than a chase is that shades will not
enter the lamp's light. Each one closes on the player until the light on it
passes a threshold, then holds at that edge and waits. So the lamp is cover,
and the fuel bar is the timer on that cover -- the same bar that was already
counting down, now with teeth.

That also means the fight and the errand pull in opposite directions. Firing a
flare spends the fuel that is keeping them off you.
~#
class Shade {
	@at : Vector3;
	@body : Prop;
	@alive : Bool;
	# Counts up while dying, so a kill fades rather than vanishing mid-frame.
	@fade : Float;
	@bob : Float;
	# Where it started. A shade driven back by a flare returns here rather than
	# to the player, which gives the room a rhythm instead of a constant press.
	@home : Vector3;

	New(x : Float, y : Float, z : Float) {
		@at := Vector3->New(x, y, z);
		@home := Vector3->New(x, y, z);
		@alive := true;
		@fade := 0.0;
		@bob := x + z;
	}

	method : public : AddTo(scene : Scene, mesh : Mesh, material : Material) ~ Nil {
		@body := scene->AddProp(Prop->New(mesh, material));
		@body->GetTransform()->SetScale(0.42);
		@body->GetTransform()->SetPosition(@at->GetX(), @at->GetY(), @at->GetZ());
	}

	method : public : IsAlive() ~ Bool { return @alive; }
	method : public : GetAt() ~ Vector3 { return @at; }
	method : public : GetProp() ~ Prop { return @body; }

	#~
	Distance from a point on the floor, height ignored -- same reasoning as the
	relics: looking up must not change how close anything is.
	~#
	method : public : GroundDistance(eye : Vector3) ~ Float {
		dx := @at->GetX() - eye->GetX();
		dz := @at->GetZ() - eye->GetZ();
		return (dx * dx + dz * dz)->Sqrt();
	}

	#~
	Killed by a flare. It does not disappear here: @alive goes false so it stops
	hunting and stops counting as a threat, and the fade drives it out of sight
	over the next second.
	~#
	method : public : Kill() ~ Nil {
		@alive := false;
		@fade := 1.0;
	}

	#~
	Advance one frame.

	@param delta seconds since the last frame
	@param eye where the player is
	@param lit how far the lamp currently reaches. Shrinks as fuel burns, which
	is what lets the shades come closer late without changing their speed.
	@return true if this shade is touching the player THIS frame
	~#
	method : public : Update(delta : Float, eye : Vector3, lit : Float) ~ Bool {
		@bob += delta * 2.2;

		if(<>@alive) {
			if(@fade > 0.0) {
				@fade -= delta * 1.6;
				if(@fade <= 0.0) {
					@fade := 0.0;
					if(@body <> Nil) {
						@body->SetVisible(false);
					};
				}
				else {
					if(@body <> Nil) {
						# Sinking as it goes, so a kill reads as collapse.
						@body->GetTransform()->SetScale(0.42 * @fade);
						@body->GetTransform()->SetPosition(@at->GetX(),
							@at->GetY() - (1.0 - @fade) * 0.5, @at->GetZ());
					};
				};
			};
			return false;
		};

		distance := GroundDistance(eye);

		# The whole behaviour, in one comparison: approach while the light here
		# is weak, retreat while it is strong. `lit` is the radius the lamp
		# actually reaches, so a guttering lamp lets them stand where a full one
		# would not.
		target_x := @home->GetX();
		target_z := @home->GetZ();
		speed := 1.15;
		if(distance > lit) {
			target_x := eye->GetX();
			target_z := eye->GetZ();
			speed := 1.55;
		};

		dx := target_x - @at->GetX();
		dz := target_z - @at->GetZ();
		length := (dx * dx + dz * dz)->Sqrt();
		if(length > 0.05) {
			step := speed * delta;
			@at->Set(@at->GetX() + dx / length * step, @at->GetY(),
				@at->GetZ() + dz / length * step);
		};

		if(@body <> Nil) {
			lift := 0.09 * @bob->Sin();
			@body->GetTransform()->SetPosition(@at->GetX(), @at->GetY() + lift, @at->GetZ());
			@body->GetTransform()->SetRotation(@bob * 0.35, 0.0, 0.0);
		};

		# 1.5, not touching-distance: a shade that has closed to arm's length is
		# already on you, and requiring overlap made the threat read as a
		# collision bug rather than a threat.
		return distance < 1.5;
	}
}

class Lantern {
	@window : GLWindow;
	@shader : Shader;
	@cube : Mesh;
	@orb : Mesh;

	@wall_texture : Texture2D;
	@floor_texture : Texture2D;
	@relic_texture : Texture2D;
	# Two materials for the same mesh: the solid relic, and the barely-opaque
	# shell drawn over it.
	@relic_material : Material;
	@glow : Material;

	@camera : Camera;
	@scene : Scene;
	@rig : LightRig;
	@lamp : Light;
	@shadows : PointShadow;

	@relics : Relic[];
	@shades : Shade[];
	# The sphere the shades are drawn with, and the flare's impact sparks.
	@shade_material : Material;
	@sparks : Particles;

	# Combat state. @health is the only way to lose that is not the clock.
	@health : Float;
	@lost : Bool;
	@kills : Int;
	# Counts down after a flare, so holding the button is not a beam.
	@flare_cooldown : Float;
	# Both drive a one-frame HUD flash and then decay.
	@hurt_flash : Float;
	@flare_flash : Float;
	# Reused every frame rather than allocated: the lamp moves with the player.
	@lamp_at : Vector3;
	@carried : Int;
	@exit_at : Vector3;
	@won : Bool;

	@overlay : Overlay;
	# Synthesised at startup rather than shipped, so this example needs no asset
	# files. Nil when the machine has no working audio output, which is normal on
	# a CI runner and is not a reason to refuse to run.
	@chime : MixChunk;

	@fuel : Float;
	@fuel_max : Float;
	@radius : Float;
	@sensitivity : Float;

	# Per-frame scratch. Every one of these exists so the draw loop allocates
	# nothing: the framework's *Into forms write into a caller's array, and a
	# Float->New[16] per object per frame is a few hundred arrays a second.
	@view_projection : Float[];

	# FPS is turned into a string four times a second rather than sixty. Building
	# it every frame both allocates and defeats Overlay's glyph cache, since the
	# text changes every time it is asked to draw it.
	@hud_timer : Float;
	@hud_fps : String;

	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		Lantern->New()->Run();
	}

	New() {
		@camera := Camera->New(Vector3->New(0.0, 1.6, 9.0));
		@lamp_at := Vector3->New(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);

		@carried := 0;
		@won := false;
		@fuel_max := 95.0;
		@fuel := @fuel_max;
		@radius := 0.35;
		@sensitivity := 0.0022;
		@hud_timer := 0.0;
		@hud_fps := "";
		@exit_at := Vector3->New(0.0, 0.0, 10.5);

		@health := 100.0;
		@lost := false;
		@kills := 0;
		@flare_cooldown := 0.0;
		@hurt_flash := 0.0;
		@flare_flash := 0.0;
	}

	#~
	How far the lamp meaningfully reaches right now.

	Not the Light's range, which is fixed: this is the radius the shades treat
	as safe, and it shrinks with the fuel. At full fuel they are held well back;
	at a quarter they are close enough to matter; at zero the lamp is out and
	nothing is holding them at all.
	~#
	method : LitRadius() ~ Float {
		# Straight down to zero, with NO floor under it. An earlier version kept a
		# 1.6 minimum, which quietly made the whole fight impossible: shades stop
		# at this radius and reach you at 1.5, so a floor above 1.5 meant they
		# could never touch you while a drop of fuel remained.
		#
		# 4.8 at full, so they are held at the edge of sight and visible there,
		# and under 1.5 at about a third of a tank -- which is where the game
		# turns from an errand into a problem.
		return 4.8 * (@fuel / @fuel_max);
	}

	method : Run() ~ Nil {
		# 4x multisampling, and audio. Both are refused gracefully: the window
		# opens without antialiasing rather than failing, and HasAudio reports
		# whether the device actually opened.
		@window := GLWindow->New("Lantern", 1024, 640, 4, true);
		if(<>@window->IsOk()) {
			@window->GetError()->ErrorLine();
			return;
		};

		leaving {
			Close();
		};

		if(<>Build()) {
			return;
		};

		"Lantern -- four relics, one lamp, and it is burning down."->PrintLine();
		"WASD to walk, mouse to look, E to take a relic, escape to quit."->PrintLine();

		# Relative mouse mode locks the cursor and keeps reporting movement past
		# the screen edge, which is what a look control needs.
		Cursor->SetRelativeMouseMode(true);

		while(<>@window->PollEvents()) {
			@window->BeginFrame();
			delta := @window->GetDelta();

			@camera->Turn(@window->GetMouseDeltaX() * @sensitivity * -1.0);
			@camera->Look(@window->GetMouseDeltaY() * @sensitivity * -1.0);

			Update(delta);
			Draw();

			@window->EndFrame();
		};

		Cursor->SetRelativeMouseMode(false);
		GL->ReportErrors("the lantern");
	}

	method : Update(delta : Float) ~ Nil {
		# Both endings freeze the world but keep drawing it, so the last frame
		# stays on screen with the message over it.
		if(@won | @lost) {
			return;
		};

		if(@flare_cooldown > 0.0) { @flare_cooldown -= delta; };
		if(@hurt_flash > 0.0) { @hurt_flash -= delta * 2.0; };
		if(@flare_flash > 0.0) { @flare_flash -= delta * 4.0; };

		speed := 3.1;
		if(@window->KeyHeld(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_LSHIFT,
				Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_RSHIFT)) {
			speed := 5.6;
		};
		step := speed * delta;

		forward := 0.0;
		if(@window->KeyHeld(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_W)) { forward += step; };
		if(@window->KeyHeld(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_S)) { forward -= step; };

		strafe := 0.0;
		if(@window->KeyHeld(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_D)) { strafe += step; };
		if(@window->KeyHeld(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_A)) { strafe -= step; };

		if(forward <> 0.0 | strafe <> 0.0) {
			# Each axis applied separately, so a glancing hit on a corridor wall
			# slides along it instead of stopping dead.
			@scene->SlideMove(@camera, forward, strafe, @radius);
		};

		# An EDGE, not a held state: MousePressed and WasPressed are true for one
		# frame per press. Reading the key as held instead -- which is all this
		# could do before -- fired TakeNearbyRelic sixty times a second for as
		# long as the key was down, and only the already-taken check hid it.
		if(@window->WasPressed(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_E)) {
			TakeNearbyRelic();
		};

		# Left mouse, or space, throws a flare. Also an EDGE: the cooldown would
		# rate-limit it anyway, but reading a held button here would drain the
		# lamp in a second the first time somebody leaned on it.
		if(@window->MousePressed(1) | @window->WasPressed(Scancode->SDL_SCANCODE_SPACE)) {
			Flare();
		};

		# The lamp burns whether or not you are moving, which is what makes
		# wandering expensive and the shortest route worth finding.
		@fuel -= delta * 1.0;
		if(@fuel < 0.0) {
			@fuel := 0.0;
		};

		each(i : @relics) {
			@relics[i]->Animate(delta);
		};

		# The shades move against the light the fuel is paying for, so this has
		# to run after the fuel has been spent this frame, not before.
		lit := LitRadius();
		here := @camera->GetPosition();
		touching := false;
		each(i : @shades) {
			if(@shades[i]->Update(delta, here, lit)) {
				touching := true;
			};
		};

		if(touching) {
			# Per second, not per contact: several shades on you is worse than
			# one, but a single one is a countdown rather than an instant loss.
			@health -= delta * 26.0;
			@hurt_flash := 1.0;
			if(@health <= 0.0) {
				@health := 0.0;
				@lost := true;
				"The dark closed over you."->PrintLine();
			};
		};

		if(@sparks <> Nil) {
			@sparks->Update(delta);
		};

		CheckExit();
	}

	#~
	A flare: a burst of light down the line you are looking at, which costs the
	lamp to throw.

	It is the same hitscan the shooting-gallery example uses -- Scene->Raycast
	reports the NEAREST thing, so a shade behind a pillar is safe from it, and
	the walls stop the shot without this method knowing walls exist.

	The cost is the design. Every flare is fuel not spent on keeping them back,
	so clearing a room makes the next room darker.
	~#
	method : Flare() ~ Nil {
		if(@flare_cooldown > 0.0 | @fuel <= 0.0) {
			return;
		};

		@flare_cooldown := 0.45;
		@flare_flash := 1.0;
		@fuel -= 6.0;
		if(@fuel < 0.0) {
			@fuel := 0.0;
		};

		hit := @scene->RaycastFrom(@camera, 40.0);
		if(<>hit->IsHit()) {
			return;
		};

		if(@sparks <> Nil) {
			# Pushed back along the surface normal so the burst sits in front of
			# whatever stopped it rather than inside it.
			@sparks->Burst(hit->GetX() + hit->GetNormalX() * 0.05,
				hit->GetY() + hit->GetNormalY() * 0.05,
				hit->GetZ() + hit->GetNormalZ() * 0.05, 10, 2.4);
		};

		prop := hit->GetProp();
		if(prop = Nil) {
			# Stone. The flare still lit it, which is the consolation.
			return;
		};

		each(i : @shades) {
			shade := @shades[i];
			if(shade->IsAlive() & shade->GetProp() = prop) {
				shade->Kill();
				@kills += 1;
				if(@chime <> Nil) {
					@chime->PlayChannel(-1, 0);
				};
			};
		};
	}

	method : TakeNearbyRelic() ~ Nil {
		here := @camera->GetPosition();
		each(i : @relics) {
			relic := @relics[i];
			if(<>relic->IsTaken() & relic->GroundDistance(here) < 1.3) {
				relic->Take();
				@carried += 1;
				if(@chime <> Nil) {
					# -1 picks any free channel, so two quick pickups overlap
					# rather than cutting each other off.
					@chime->PlayChannel(-1, 0);
				};
				left := @relics->Size() - @carried;
				"Relic taken -- {$left} still out there."->PrintLine();
				# A relic is worth a little fuel, so collecting is also survival.
				@fuel += 12.0;
				if(@fuel > @fuel_max) {
					@fuel := @fuel_max;
				};
			};
		};
	}

	method : CheckExit() ~ Nil {
		if(@carried < @relics->Size()) {
			return;
		};

		here := @camera->GetPosition();
		dx := @exit_at->GetX() - here->GetX();
		dz := @exit_at->GetZ() - here->GetZ();
		if((dx * dx + dz * dz)->Sqrt() < 1.6) {
			@won := true;
		};
	}

	method : Draw() ~ Nil {
		# The lantern is HELD OUT, to the right and a little ahead. That is not
		# decoration, it is the whole feature working or not.
		#
		# A light at the eye casts no visible shadow: every shadow falls directly
		# behind the thing casting it, exactly where that thing hides it. Measured
		# on this scene -- a horizontal scan across a wall with a pillar in front
		# of it read a smooth bright hump, 138 up to 255 and back to 161, with no
		# dark band anywhere. Moving the lamp 0.6 to one side put a hard notch in
		# the same scan, 84 down to 3. Same shadow map, same bias; only the offset
		# changed.
		eye := @camera->GetPosition();
		right := @camera->GroundRight();
		ahead := @camera->GroundForward();
		@lamp_at->Set(
			eye->GetX() + right->GetX() * 0.55 + ahead->GetX() * 0.35,
			eye->GetY() - 0.2,
			eye->GetZ() + right->GetZ() * 0.55 + ahead->GetZ() * 0.35);
		@lamp->SetPosition(@lamp_at);

		# Intensity is low and the range is short, because the readings above also
		# showed the first numbers here saturating every surface to 255 -- at which
		# point a shadow cannot darken anything and the dungeon is not dark.
		fuel_light := @fuel / @fuel_max;
		@lamp->SetIntensity(0.12 + fuel_light * 0.20);

		# Pass one: six faces of depth, from the lamp. This is the expensive part
		# -- every caster is submitted six times -- and it is why the room is
		# built from as few boxes as it can be.
		@shadows->SetLight(@lamp);
		@shadows->Capture(@scene, @window);

		# Pass two: the scene from the eye, reading that cube.
		# Cheap when nothing changed, and correct when it did: a window can be
		# resized at any moment, and a projection built once at startup then
		# stretches the scene while the pixel-space HUD stays right.
		@camera->SetAspect(@window->GetAspect());
		@view_projection := @camera->GetViewProjection();

		# Only what Scene does not do for itself. Scene->Draw already calls Use,
		# SetInt("tex") and the light rig -- so the specular needs the eye
		# position, and the shadow map has no setter on Scene and must be
		# applied here. The other three lines were duplicating the scene.
		@rig->SetViewPosition(eye);
		@shadows->ApplyTo(@shader);

		# One call. The relics are props in this scene now, so they are drawn,
		# culled and depth-sorted with everything else rather than by hand
		# afterwards -- and their glow shells sort against the room's own
		# transparent geometry instead of being blended on at the end.
		@scene->SetEye(eye);
		@scene->Draw(@view_projection);

		# After the scene, because the sparks are additive and must blend over
		# what is already there rather than be depth-tested against it.
		if(@sparks <> Nil) {
			@sparks->Draw(@view_projection);
		};

		DrawHud();
	}

	method : DrawHud() ~ Nil {
		if(@overlay = Nil) {
			return;
		};

		@hud_timer += @window->GetDelta();
		if(@hud_timer > 0.25) {
			@hud_timer := 0.0;
			fps := @window->GetFps()->As(Int);
			drawn := @scene->GetDrawnCount();
			culled := @scene->GetCulledCount();
			@hud_fps := "{$fps} fps   {$drawn} drawn / {$culled} culled";
		};

		total := @relics->Size();
		# Interpolation reads locals, not instance variables.
		carried := @carried;

		@overlay->Begin();

		# The bar carries the reading twice over: its width, and now its colour.
		# Green through amber to red as the lamp burns down, so the state is
		# legible without reading anything.
		fraction := @fuel / @fuel_max;
		bar := (200.0 * fraction)->As(Int);

		# The trough, so an empty bar is still visibly a bar rather than nothing.
		@overlay->Rect(16, 44, 200, 10, 38, 34, 30);
		if(bar > 0) {
			red := 220;
			green := 60;
			if(fraction > 0.25) {
				# 0.25..1.0 mapped onto amber..green
				lift := (fraction - 0.25) / 0.75;
				red := 220 - (140.0 * lift)->As(Int);
				green := 60 + (150.0 * lift)->As(Int);
			};
			@overlay->Rect(16, 44, bar, 10, red, green, 70);
		};

		# Health under the fuel bar, and deliberately a different colour: the two
		# bars mean different things and must not be read as one.
		health_fraction := @health / 100.0;
		health_bar := (200.0 * health_fraction)->As(Int);
		@overlay->Rect(16, 60, 200, 10, 38, 30, 34);
		if(health_bar > 0) {
			@overlay->Rect(16, 60, health_bar, 10, 200, 70, 90);
		};

		kills := @kills;
		@overlay->Text("relics {$carried} / {$total}    shades {$kills}", 16, 16);

		# The crosshair. Two short lines rather than a texture, and it brightens
		# for the frames after a flare so a shot has an acknowledgement even when
		# it hits nothing.
		cx := @window->GetWidth() / 2;
		cy := @window->GetHeight() / 2;
		# Line takes a THICKNESS, not a colour -- the colour is overlay state, and
		# SetTintRgb is the cheap way to set it. SetColor would work too but
		# throws away the glyph cache, and the HUD text is drawn from the same
		# overlay a few lines above.
		if(@flare_flash > 0.0) {
			@overlay->SetTintRgb(255, 230, 180);
		}
		else {
			@overlay->SetTintRgb(150, 150, 150);
		};
		@overlay->Line(cx - 9, cy, cx - 3, cy, 2);
		@overlay->Line(cx + 3, cy, cx + 9, cy, 2);
		@overlay->Line(cx, cy - 9, cx, cy - 3, 2);
		@overlay->Line(cx, cy + 3, cx, cy + 9, 2);
		@overlay->SetTintRgb(255, 255, 255);

		if(@hurt_flash > 0.0) {
			# A red edge rather than a full-screen wash, so it reads as damage
			# without hiding the thing that is doing it.
			@overlay->Rect(0, 0, @window->GetWidth(), 6, 190, 40, 50);
		};

		if(@lost) {
			@overlay->Text("The dark closed over you. Escape to quit.", 16, 84);
		}
		else if(@won) {
			@overlay->Text("You are out. All four, and the lamp still lit.", 16, 68);
		}
		else if(@fuel <= 0.0) {
			@overlay->Text("The lamp is out. You can still feel your way.", 16, 68);
		}
		else if(fraction < 0.25) {
			@overlay->Text("The lamp is guttering.", 16, 68);
		}
		else if(@carried = total) {
			@overlay->Text("All four. Back to the entrance.", 16, 84);
		};

		# Dimmer than the rest, because a frame counter is not what you are
		# playing. One call, no re-rasterising -- SetColor here would throw away
		# every cached glyph on screen twice a frame.
		@overlay->SetTintRgb(120, 130, 145);
		@overlay->Text(@hud_fps, 16, @window->GetHeight() - 30);

		@overlay->End();
	}

	method : Build() ~ Bool {
		@camera->SetPerspective(Float->Pi() / 3.0, @window->GetAspect(), 0.1, 60.0);
		GL->Enable(Capability->GL_CULL_FACE);
		GL->ClearColor(0.015, 0.017, 0.025, 1.0);

		@shader := @window->Own(Shader->LitTexturedPointShadowed())->As(Shader);
		if(<>@shader->IsOk()) {
			@shader->GetLog()->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};

		@cube := @window->Own(Mesh->Cube())->As(Mesh);
		@orb := @window->Own(Mesh->Sphere(18, 10))->As(Mesh);
		if(<>@cube->IsOk() | <>@orb->IsOk()) {
			"Could not upload the geometry"->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};

		@wall_texture := @window->Own(Texture2D->Checker(64, 4, Texture2D->Rgb(96, 88, 78),
			Texture2D->Rgb(66, 60, 54), TextureWrap->GL_REPEAT))->As(Texture2D);
		@floor_texture := @window->Own(Texture2D->Checker(64, 3, Texture2D->Rgb(58, 54, 50),
			Texture2D->Rgb(44, 41, 38), TextureWrap->GL_REPEAT))->As(Texture2D);
		@relic_texture := @window->Own(Texture2D->Solid(0xFFF2C879))->As(Texture2D);
		@relic_material := Material->New(@relic_texture);
		@glow := Material->New(@relic_texture);
		@glow->SetTint(1.0, 0.86, 0.5);
		# Set ONCE, here. It used to be set per frame in DrawRelics; converting the
		# relics to Props deleted that method and took this line with it, leaving
		# an opaque gold shell wrapped around each relic and the whole transparent
		# pass unreachable.
		@glow->SetOpacity(0.22);
		if(<>@wall_texture->IsOk() | <>@floor_texture->IsOk() | <>@relic_texture->IsOk()) {
			"Could not build the textures"->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};

		@shadows := @window->Own(PointShadow->New(512))->As(PointShadow);
		if(<>@shadows->IsOk()) {
			@shadows->GetError()->ErrorLine();
			return false;
		};
		# A lamp inside the room is close to what it lights, so the bias that suits
		# a distant sun is far too large here and detaches every shadow from its
		# caster.
		@shadows->SetBias(0.06);

		BuildAudio();
		BuildLight();
		BuildDungeon();
		BuildRelics();
		BuildShades();
		BuildSparks();
		BuildOverlay();

		return true;
	}

	#~
	A short bell, written to a temporary WAV and loaded back.

	Two sine partials a fifth apart with a fast attack and a long exponential
	decay -- enough to read as "you picked something up" without shipping an
	asset. The file goes to the system temp directory rather than beside the
	program, because the runners start it from the deploy tree's bin and that
	should not accumulate files.
	~#
	method : BuildAudio() ~ Nil {
		if(<>@window->HasAudio()) {
			return;
		};

		path := System.Runtime->GetTempDir() + "/objeck_lantern_chime.wav";
		if(WriteChime(path)) {
			@chime := MixChunk->New(path);
			if(@chime <> Nil & @chime->IsNull()) {
				@chime := Nil;
			};
		};
	}

	method : WriteChime(file : String) ~ Bool {
		rate := 22050;
		count := 5200;
		data_size := count * 2;

		writer := FileWriter->New(file);
		if(<>writer->IsOpen()) {
			return false;
		};

		# RIFF/WAVE, 16-bit mono. Written by hand because there is no WAV writer
		# in the standard library and the header is twelve fields.
		writer->WriteByte(82); writer->WriteByte(73); writer->WriteByte(70); writer->WriteByte(70);
		WriteU32(writer, 36 + data_size);
		writer->WriteByte(87); writer->WriteByte(65); writer->WriteByte(86); writer->WriteByte(69);
		writer->WriteByte(102); writer->WriteByte(109); writer->WriteByte(116); writer->WriteByte(32);
		WriteU32(writer, 16);
		WriteU16(writer, 1);
		WriteU16(writer, 1);
		WriteU32(writer, rate);
		WriteU32(writer, rate * 2);
		WriteU16(writer, 2);
		WriteU16(writer, 16);
		writer->WriteByte(100); writer->WriteByte(97); writer->WriteByte(116); writer->WriteByte(97);
		WriteU32(writer, data_size);

		two_pi := 2.0 * Float->Pi();
		rate_f := rate->As(Float);
		count_f := count->As(Float);
		low := 0.0;
		high := 0.0;

		for(i := 0; i < count; i += 1;) {
			t := i->As(Float) / count_f;
			low += two_pi * 880.0 / rate_f;
			high += two_pi * 1320.0 / rate_f;

			# Fast attack, long decay. Without the attack ramp the first sample
			# jumps from silence and the speaker clicks.
			envelope := (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t);
			if(t < 0.01) {
				envelope := envelope * (t / 0.01);
			};

			value := envelope * (0.62 * low->Sin() + 0.38 * high->Sin());
			sample := (value * 20000.0)->As(Int);
			if(sample > 32767) { sample := 32767; };
			if(sample < -32768) { sample := -32768; };
			if(sample < 0) { sample := sample + 65536; };

			writer->WriteByte(sample % 256);
			writer->WriteByte((sample / 256) % 256);
		};

		writer->Close();

		return true;
	}

	method : WriteU32(writer : FileWriter, value : Int) ~ Nil {
		writer->WriteByte(value % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 256) % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 65536) % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 16777216) % 256);
	}

	method : WriteU16(writer : FileWriter, value : Int) ~ Nil {
		writer->WriteByte(value % 256);
		writer->WriteByte((value / 256) % 256);
	}

	method : BuildLight() ~ Nil {
		# Range, not just colour: a point light with no falloff lights the whole
		# dungeon evenly and there is nothing to carry a lantern for.
		@lamp := Light->Point(Vector3->New(0.0, 1.4, 9.0), 13.0);
		@lamp->SetColor(1.0, 0.86, 0.62);

		@rig := LightRig->New();
		# Barely above black. This is the number that decides whether the game is
		# about the lantern or merely lit by one.
		@rig->SetAmbient(0.035, 0.035, 0.045);
		# Low and broad: dungeon stone is not polished, and a tight lobe on a
		# carried light turns every surface into a mirror pointed back at you.
		@rig->SetSpecular(12.0, 0.08);
		@rig->Add(@lamp);
	}

	#~
	The dungeon: a floor, a ceiling, an outer wall, and enough interior to make
	corridors and corners.

	Corners are the interesting shape here -- for the shadows, because a corner is
	where a moving light's shadow sweeps fastest and any inconsistency between
	cube faces shows; and for SlideMove, because an inside corner is where naive
	collision traps you.
	~#
	method : BuildDungeon() ~ Nil {
		@scene := Scene->New(@cube, @shader);
		@scene->SetLightRig(@rig);
		@scene->SetCulling(true);

		# Floor and ceiling: drawn, but walked through rather than into.
		Slab(0.0, -0.25, 0.0, 13.0, 0.25, 13.0, @floor_texture, false);
		Slab(0.0, 3.25, 0.0, 13.0, 0.25, 13.0, @wall_texture, false);

		# Outer wall, four slabs, leaving the south side open as the way out.
		Slab(0.0, 1.5, -13.0, 13.0, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-13.0, 1.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.5, 13.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(13.0, 1.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.5, 13.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-7.5, 1.5, 13.0, 5.5, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(7.5, 1.5, 13.0, 5.5, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);

		# Interior: two long spines with gaps, and a knot of pillars in the middle.
		Slab(-5.0, 1.5, -6.0, 0.5, 1.5, 5.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(5.0, 1.5, -6.0, 0.5, 1.5, 5.0, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-2.0, 1.5, 1.0, 4.5, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(6.5, 1.5, 1.0, 4.0, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(0.0, 1.5, -11.0, 3.0, 1.5, 0.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(-9.0, 1.5, 5.0, 0.5, 1.5, 3.5, @wall_texture, true);
		Slab(9.0, 1.5, 5.0, 0.5, 1.5, 3.5, @wall_texture, true);

		# Pillars. Small, plentiful, and the best shadow casters in the room --
		# a lantern carried past one sweeps its shadow right around the walls.
		Pillar(-2.5, -3.0);
		Pillar(2.5, -3.0);
		Pillar(-2.5, 5.5);
		Pillar(2.5, 5.5);
		Pillar(-7.0, -1.0);
		Pillar(7.0, -1.0);
		Pillar(0.0, 8.0);
	}

	method : Slab(cx : Float, cy : Float, cz : Float, hx : Float, hy : Float,
			hz : Float, texture : Texture2D, solid : Bool) ~ Nil {
		box := Box->New(Vector3->New(cx, cy, cz), Vector3->New(hx, hy, hz), texture);
		box->SetSolid(solid);
		@scene->Add(box);
	}

	method : Pillar(x : Float, z : Float) ~ Nil {
		Slab(x, 1.5, z, 0.4, 1.5, 0.4, @wall_texture, true);
	}

	method : BuildRelics() ~ Nil {
		# One per quadrant, each behind something, so none is visible from the
		# entrance and the lantern has to be carried to it.
		@relics := Relic->New[4];
		@relics[0] := Relic->New(-9.5, 1.0, -9.0);
		@relics[1] := Relic->New(9.5, 1.0, -9.5);
		@relics[2] := Relic->New(-10.0, 1.0, 8.5);
		@relics[3] := Relic->New(10.0, 1.0, 9.0);

		# Each relic joins the scene as two props -- the solid body and the glow
		# shell. The sphere is shared by all eight and owned by none of them; the
		# window frees it.
		each(i : @relics) {
			@relics[i]->AddTo(@scene, @orb, @relic_material, @glow);
		};
	}

	#~
	The shades. Fixed positions rather than random ones, so the room plays the
	same way twice and a change in behaviour is a change in the code rather than
	the draw of the dice.

	They start in the corners the lamp reaches last.
	~#
	method : BuildShades() ~ Nil {
		@shade_material := Material->New(@relic_texture);
		# Cold and dark: they have to be visible against black stone without
		# looking like another relic to walk up to.
		@shade_material->SetTint(0.30, 0.42, 0.65);

		@shades := Shade->New[4];
		@shades[0] := Shade->New(-9.5, 0.9, -9.5);
		@shades[1] := Shade->New(9.5, 0.9, -9.5);
		@shades[2] := Shade->New(-9.5, 0.9, 4.0);
		@shades[3] := Shade->New(9.5, 0.9, 4.0);

		each(i : @shades) {
			@shades[i]->AddTo(@scene, @orb, @shade_material);
		};
	}

	#~
	The flare's impact sparks: one pooled, camera-facing, additively blended
	batch, the same shape the shooting gallery uses. Warmer and shorter-lived
	than its sparks, because this one is a flare rather than debris.
	~#
	method : BuildSparks() ~ Nil {
		@sparks := @window->Own(Particles->New(64))->As(Particles);
		@sparks->SetCamera(@camera);
		@sparks->SetLifetime(0.5);
		@sparks->SetSize(0.42, 0.0);
		@sparks->SetGravity(-3.0);
		@sparks->SetDrag(2.0);
		@sparks->SetAdditive(true);

		glow := Material->New(@relic_texture);
		glow->SetTint(1.0, 0.80, 0.45);
		@sparks->SetMaterial(glow);
	}

	method : BuildOverlay() ~ Nil {
		# One call. It finds the shipped font whether this is started from a
		# deploy tree's bin or from the examples directory it ships in -- the
		# hardcoded relative path this replaced only worked from the first.
		@overlay := @window->Own(Overlay->WithDefaultFont(@window, 18))->As(Overlay);
		if(@overlay->IsOk() & @overlay->HasFont()) {
			@overlay->SetColor(236, 224, 198);
		}
		else {
			# A missing font is not fatal: the game is playable without the HUD,
			# and DrawHud checks for this.
			@overlay := Nil;
		};
	}

	#~
	GL objects first, while the context is still current -- every Free bottoms out
	in a glDelete*, which needs one. The window takes the context and SDL down
	after them.

	Six objects and a fixed order, written out by hand, in every example in this
	directory. A GLWindow->Own registry would do it once.
	~#
	#~
	What a teardown looks like once the window owns things.

	This used to be eight lines of `if(x <> Nil) { x->Free(); }` in an order that
	mattered and was documented nowhere. Every GL object here was handed to
	window->Own as it was built, so the window releases all of it -- before
	destroying the context, which is the one rule that actually matters.

	The chime is not a GL object and the window knows nothing about it, so it
	still goes by hand, and still before the window: GLWindow->Free calls
	Core->Quit, which takes the audio device with it.
	~#
	method : Close() ~ Nil {
		if(@chime <> Nil) { @chime->Free(); };

		if(@window <> Nil) { @window->Free(); };
	}
}
